#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:46
msgid "Passwords and email addresses are checked against public data breach corpora on login and profile update."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:48
msgid "Detect if your users are logging in with a password or email that appears in public data leaks."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:75
msgid "Breach Check is disabled"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:76
msgid "The first cause of account takeover today is credential reuse: users logging in with a password that already leaked somewhere else. This module detects that condition passively, without blocking anyone."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:81
msgid "When a user logs in, Login Armor checks the password against the public Have I Been Pwned corpus using a privacy-preserving range lookup: only the first 5 characters of a one-way hash ever leave your server. If a match is found, the user gets a friendly warning on their profile page inviting them to change their password. Admins see the count of flagged users on the overview."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:84
msgid "Zero dependencies, zero keys."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:89
msgid "The APIs are free and keyless. Passwords never leave the server in readable form. The optional email check, off by default, sends the email address itself to XposedOrNot."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:99
msgid "Enable Breach Check"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:120
msgid "What gets checked, and when"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:122
msgid "Email addresses:"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:123
msgid "every administrator account is tested against the public breach list the moment you enable the email check. The counters below update straight away."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:126
msgid "Passwords:"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:127
msgid "existing passwords cannot be tested in bulk. WordPress only keeps a one-way fingerprint of each password, which by design cannot be reversed into the original text. Each password is therefore tested the next time its user logs in, while WordPress briefly handles the plaintext to authenticate them. No login is ever blocked. A flagged user just sees a friendly heads-up on their profile page, inviting them to change the password."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:136
msgid "Password check"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:138
msgid "On each successful login, check the password against the Have I Been Pwned corpus via its k-anonymity range API. Only the first 5 characters of a SHA-1 hash of the password leave your server: the password itself is never transmitted, not even in hashed form."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:152
msgid "Check passwords on login and profile update"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:160
msgid "Email check"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:162
msgid "When a new user is created or an email is changed, check the address against the XposedOrNot public breach list. The API returns the names of the breaches that contain the address, so users can know which passwords they may have reused."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:163
msgid "Unlike the password check, this sends the email address itself to XposedOrNot."
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:176
msgid "Check email addresses on user creation and email change"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:184
msgid "Force password reset for flagged users"
msgstr ""

#: admin/views/sections/breach-check.php:186
msgid "When on, a user whose password has been flagged as breached is automatically redirected to their profile page on every admin screen until they change the password. The user is not locked out: they can still access their account, they are just nagged until the issue is resolved. Off by default; turn on if you want zero-tolerance enforcement."
